libhsakmt (fmm): never drop the CPU mapping of a released host-VA range
(gfx803, ROCm 10.0)

WHY
---
`reserved_aperture_release()` in projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c
munmaps a released CPU-accessible host-VA range and re-reserves it as
PROT_NONE "to release shmem pages for MAP_SHARED allocations". Releasing
the *userspace* handle is not the end of that range's life: the kernel
keeps the KFD userptr BO (created by `hsa_amd_memory_lock`, e.g. PyTorch's
H2D staging windows) mapped in GPUVM until it is explicitly destroyed.
Any GPU work still queued behind the unmap reads a VA whose PTEs the
eviction path has already torn down -> VM_CONTEXT1_PROTECTION_FAULT on a
live buffer.

Reproduces deterministically on gfx803 loading ANY sd1.5-class checkpoint
through torch (`safetensors.get_tensor` + `.to("cuda")` in a loop; ~2
faults per 25 s pass, 100% of runs) and was the root cause of the
intermittent ComfyUI SIGSEGV/hang on the 10.0 line. The same race exists
on every architecture, but there the kernel recovers via userptr
eviction/restore (upstream's own comment in `__fmm_release` warns that
"munmap would cause an eviction"); on CIK the queues cannot be preempted
reliably, so one fault wedges the CP: "Queue preemption failed",
"Failed to quiesce KFD", GPU-reset cascades, and processes that hang at
`DQM create queue` (this is the gpu_recovery=0 stall captured end-to-end
on real Polaris hardware during this investigation).

Keeping the mapping is exactly what the deferred-release FIFO already
does for parked objects; the shmem-page reclaim it buys is not worth a
wedged GPU.

FIX
---
Drop the munmap()+mmap(PROT_NONE) pair from `reserved_aperture_release()`;
the mbind() NUMA-policy reset stays. The VA range remains mapped (and
userspace-reserved in fmm's vm tree) until process exit.

Verified on RX 580 (gfx803), kernel 7.1.12 Fedora stock (gpu_recovery=1):
before - 2 PROTECTION_FAULTs + resets on every repro run; after - 0 faults
and 0 resets over 4x torch-load repros, 5x ComfyUI checkpoint loads, and a
full sd1.5 txt2img generation (6 steps, EXIT=0).

Applies with: git apply (rocm-systems is cloned as a real git repo).

--- a/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c
+++ b/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c
@@ -707,24 +707,17 @@
 	}
 
 	if (app->is_cpu_accessible) {
-		void *mmap_ret;
-
 		/* Reset NUMA policy */
 		mbind(address, MemorySizeInBytes, MPOL_DEFAULT, NULL, 0, 0);
 
-		/*
-		 * Drop the CPU mapping to release shmem pages for MAP_SHARED
-		 * allocations, then re-reserve the VA range as PROT_NONE.
-		 * Callers hold fmm_mutex (see contract above), which serializes
-		 * this against aperture allocations, so no allocation can claim
-		 * the VA in the window between munmap() and mmap().
+		/* GFX803_FMM_KEEP_USERPTR_MAP: do NOT munmap here. A KFD
+		 * userptr (hsa_amd_memory_lock) keeps the kernel mapping of
+		 * this range alive after userspace released it, and queued
+		 * GPU work still reads it. Unmapping now evicts those PTEs
+		 * under the GPU; on gfx803 the resulting VM fault cannot be
+		 * recovered without a CP reset. Keep the mapping (the mbind
+		 * above already resets the NUMA policy).
 		 */
-		munmap(address, MemorySizeInBytes);
-		mmap_ret = mmap(address, MemorySizeInBytes, PROT_NONE,
-			MAP_ANONYMOUS | MAP_NORESERVE | MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_FIXED,
-			-1, 0);
-		if (mmap_ret == MAP_FAILED)
-			pr_err("Failed to reserve VA range %p: %s\n", address, strerror(errno));
 	}
 }
 
